{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And he did not know, yet he is guilty, and he shall bear his iniquity.</b> Rabbi Yosi the Galilean says: Scripture punished one who did not know — all the more so one who did know. Rabbi Yosi says: If you wish to know the reward of commandments, go and learn from the first man, etc.; he who restrains himself from piggulim and notarot, and who fasts on Yom Kippur, etc. (Sifra, Vayikra, §63). One must ponder why he chose precisely these three, and it appears to be because the first man stumbled in an act of forbidden eating; therefore he said the opposite — that one who is careful regarding forbidden eating has three levels. For 'he who restrains himself from piggulim' is a comprehensive term for every kind of forbidden eating, since it is piggul; and not only does he not eat forbidden food as the first man did, but even from what is permitted to him he eats only what is necessary, and distances himself from surplus, eating no extras beyond what is necessary — for the righteous eats only to satisfy his soul, for the maintenance of the body alone. And afterward he said: not only does he not eat extras, but even from what is necessary he sometimes distances himself — and this is one who fasts on Yom Kippur, in order to purge his matter in the crucible of affliction — all the more so! And from eating one may infer as well to all the other uses and needs of a person. And that Rabbi Yosi attached his words to this passage, it appears to me that he came to resolve what is written: אָשָׁם הוּא אָשֹׁם אָשַׁם לַה׳ — these three instances of אָשָׁם, what are they? Rather, he came to say that if one is guilty for having eaten piggul, all the more so is he guilty for eating surplus from what is permitted to him, and all the more so for not fasting. And from this you learn to be careful in all three, and what their reward is."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:08.481945928Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}